🧠 One real-world example

Suppose you have an EV charging dataset:

| Date  | Temperature | EV Count | Charging Demand |
| ----- | ----------: | -------: | --------------: |
| Jan 1 |          25 |      100 |             500 |
| Jan 2 |          27 |      120 |             600 |
| Jan 3 |          26 |      150 |             750 |


====================

NumPy

Perform numerical calculations:

import numpy as np

demand = np.array([500, 600, 750])

print(demand.mean())

Purpose: numerical calculation.

-----

Pandas

Load and analyze the complete table:

import pandas as pd

df = pd.read_csv("ev_data.csv")

print(df.head())

Purpose: data manipulation and analysis.

-----

Matplotlib

Visualize charging demand:

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.plot(df["Date"], df["Charging Demand"])

plt.show()

Purpose: visualization.

-----

Scikit-learn

Build a prediction model:

from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression

model = LinearRegression()

model.fit(X, y)

prediction = model.predict(new_data)

Purpose: Machine Learning.

